WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
Last time, selective loss left a small tube rich in active target.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.080
Run a sample on a gel.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.620
One clean band appears.

4
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.440
Is it one protein, and which protein is it?

5
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.427
That gel is SDS-PAGE.

6
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:24.427
The detergent SDS unfolds each chain and coats it with negative charge.

7
00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:29.327
Chains then sort by size alone, the small ones running fastest.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:35.410
A ladder of marker proteins, each of known size, runs in the next lane.

9
00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.870
Read the band against it, and you estimate the chain's size.

10
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.670
That is the gel's first answer.

11
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:46.483
Its other answer is a first look at purity.

12
00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:50.703
One band per kind of chain is needed, but not enough.

13
00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:54.303
Two proteins of the same size run together.

14
00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:58.143
A contaminant can hide below what the stain detects.

15
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.867
Here is the guiding idea.

16
00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:03.987
A band gives a size, not a name.

17
00:01:05.347 --> 00:01:07.647
Purity and identity are separate questions.

18
00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:12.227
Antibodies, masses and sequence say which protein it is.

19
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.510
First, antibodies.

20
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.750
Copy the gel onto a membrane, band for band.

21
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:23.390
Probe it with an antibody, and only the matching band lights up.

22
00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.950
A validated antibody supports identity.

23
00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:34.183
Second, mass. Trypsin cuts the protein into a predictable set of peptides.

24
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:37.683
A laser launches them down a flight tube.

25
00:01:38.943 --> 00:01:42.023
Most carry one charge, so heavier ones arrive later.

26
00:01:43.117 --> 00:01:47.437
Arrival times give each peptide's mass, to a fraction of a dalton.

27
00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:50.797
But you never read a single amino acid.

28
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:53.957
Can a list of masses name the protein?

29
00:01:60.000 --> 00:02:01.580
For a known protein, yes.

30
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.980
A computer predicts the trypsin digest of every protein in a database.

31
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.660
Few proteins give the same set of masses.

32
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.700
The list works like a fingerprint.

33
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:21.553
Third, sequence. Edman degradation clips the first residue off the chain, names it,

34
00:02:21.693 --> 00:02:26.393
and repeats. Each cycle is only about 98% % efficient.

35
00:02:27.793 --> 00:02:31.993
So long proteins are cut into overlapping peptides and read in pieces.

36
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:36.287
The modern answer is tandem mass spectrometry.

37
00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:41.807
One peptide breaks into a ladder of pieces, each one residue shorter.

38
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:46.027
Each gap between peaks matches one amino acid.

39
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.907
Leucine and isoleucine weigh the same, so blind spots remain.

40
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.680
Frederick Sanger spent 12 years reading the 51 amino acids of insulin by hand.

41
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.240
Today, mass spectrometry can identify a protein from its peptides in a single run.

42
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:08.583
So one clean band opens the question of identity.

43
00:03:09.803 --> 00:03:11.183
It does not close it.

44
00:03:12.423 --> 00:03:16.803
Size comes from the gel; the name comes from antibodies, masses or sequence.
